  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>Begin building a personal collection of fonts — both handwriting-derived faces and original designs intended for daily computer use.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:strong>Handwriting faces.</html:strong> Several distinct styles, each digitized into its own face: a gothic calligraphy variant drawn in Procreate, a print-hand variant matching everyday writing, and a set of chaotic alternates. Use these to typeset slides and other prose in my own hand.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:strong>Practical typefaces.</html:strong> Original designs for everyday reading on this machine — browser, Emacs, and (eventually) the <fr:link href="/sss-0099/" title="Forest Theming Customization" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0099/" display-uri="sss-0099" type="local">forest theme</fr:link>. Less about novelty than about making the screen look the way I want.</html:p>
    <html:p>Open questions: which tooling (FontForge, Glyphs, Birdfont, RoboFont)? What pipeline takes a Procreate-drawn glyph sheet to a usable <html:code>.woff2</html:code> / <html:code>.otf</html:code>? How many glyphs are needed for a "minimum viable" handwriting face vs. a polished one? See the fonts paragraph in <fr:link href="/sss-0099/" title="Forest Theming Customization" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0099/" display-uri="sss-0099" type="local">forest theming customization</fr:link> for the deployment side.</html:p>
    <html:p>Reference glyph sheet (example layout):</html:p>
